0c5b16bb8300a9a9fff2f63edf81","filename":"Math/Kislitsyn/docs/proof_overview.tex","status":"modified","additions":1,"deletions":1,"changes":2,"blob_url":"https://github.com/ertwro/one_third_two_thirds/blob/ccd4b3e7b3f6ab9fb94e6f9350852b2f13ba8229/Math%2FKislitsyn%2Fdocs%2Fproof_overview.tex","raw_url":"https://github.com/ertwro/one_third_two_thirds/raw/ccd4b3e7b3f6ab9fb94e6f9350852b2f13ba8229/Math%2FKislitsyn%2Fdocs%2Fproof_overview.tex","contents_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/ertwro/one_third_two_thirds/contents/Math%2FKislitsyn%2Fdocs%2Fproof_overview.tex?ref=ccd4b3e7b3f6ab9fb94e6f9350852b2f13ba8229","patch":"@@ -276,7 +276,7 @@ \\section{The Remaining Obstruction: $|S| \\ge 4$}\n \n We record two constraints that tightly circumscribe this case.\n \n-\\medskip\\noindent\\textbf{1. Minority Floor (Equation~\\ref{eq:minority-floor}).}\n+\\medskip\\noindent\\textbf{1. Minority Floor (Theorem~\\ref{thm:hypercube}(1)).}\n Each bottleneck pair's minority set receives at least $2^{s-1}$\n extensions from the hypercube, all adjacent.  This forces $k > 3\n \\cdot 2^{s-1}$ (otherwise $\\mathrm{minority} \\ge 2^{s-1} \\ge k/3$,"}]}